WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.284
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:08.920
SARA ROBINSON: Hello, everyone.

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:10.620
Welcome to Intro
to Machine Learning

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:12.570
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:14.230
My name is Sara Robinson.

00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:17.490
I'm a developer advocate on
the Google Cloud Platform Team,

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:19.600
and I focus on machine learning.

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:23.280
You can find me on
Twitter at @SRobTweets.

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:25.110
So let's dive right
in by talking about--

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:26.770
what is machine learning?

00:00:26.770 --> 00:00:28.920
So at a high level,
machine learning

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:31.800
involves teaching computers
to recognize patterns

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:33.900
in the same way
that our brains do.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:35.790
So as humans, it's
really easy for us

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:38.400
to distinguish between
a cat and a dog,

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:40.740
but it's much more
difficult to teach a machine

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:43.740
to be able to do the same thing.

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:45.490
In this talk, I'm going
to focus on what's

00:00:45.490 --> 00:00:46.840
called supervised learning.

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:48.520
This is when,
during training, you

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:51.147
give your model labeled input.

00:00:51.147 --> 00:00:53.230
And we can think of almost
any supervised learning

00:00:53.230 --> 00:00:54.640
problem in this way.

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:57.910
So we provide labeled
inputs to our model,

00:00:57.910 --> 00:01:00.290
and then our model
outputs a prediction.

00:01:00.290 --> 00:01:03.430
Now, the amount that we know
about how our model works

00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:05.290
is going to depend
on the tool we choose

00:01:05.290 --> 00:01:07.476
to use for the job and
the type of machine

00:01:07.476 --> 00:01:09.100
learning problem
we're trying to solve.

00:01:11.514 --> 00:01:12.930
So that was a high
level overview,

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:18.142
but how do we actually get
from input to description?

00:01:18.142 --> 00:01:20.350
Again, this is going to
depend on the type of machine

00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:22.462
learning problem
we're trying to solve.

00:01:22.462 --> 00:01:23.920
So on the left-hand
side, let's say

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:26.290
you're solving a generic
task that someone has already

00:01:26.290 --> 00:01:27.550
solved before.

00:01:27.550 --> 00:01:29.845
In that case, you don't
need to start from scratch.

00:01:29.845 --> 00:01:31.930
But on the other
side of the spectrum,

00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:34.090
let's say you're solving
a custom task that's

00:01:34.090 --> 00:01:35.617
very specific to your data set.

00:01:35.617 --> 00:01:37.450
Then you're going to
need to build and train

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:39.770
your own model from scratch.

00:01:39.770 --> 00:01:41.350
So more specifically,
let's think

00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:44.680
of this in terms of
image classification.

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:47.200
So let's say we want
to label this image

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:48.884
as a picture of a cat.

00:01:48.884 --> 00:01:50.800
This is a really common
machine learning task.

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:52.341
There's tons of
models out there that

00:01:52.341 --> 00:01:54.374
exist to help us label images.

00:01:54.374 --> 00:01:56.290
So we can use one of
these pre-trained models.

00:01:56.290 --> 00:02:00.036
We don't need to
start from scratch.

00:02:00.036 --> 00:02:01.410
But let's say, on
the other hand,

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:04.340
that this cat's name is
Chloe, this is our cat,

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:06.440
and we want to
identify her apart

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:10.009
from other cats across
our entire image library.

00:02:10.009 --> 00:02:12.440
So we're going to need to train
a model using our own data

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:15.350
from scratch so that it
can differentiate Chloe

00:02:15.350 --> 00:02:17.940
from other cats.

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:20.310
More specifically, let's
say that we want our model

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:24.574
to return a bounding box showing
where she is in that picture.

00:02:24.574 --> 00:02:26.990
We're also going to need to
train a model on our own data.

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:32.190
Let's also think about this
in terms of a natural language

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:34.820
processing problem.

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:38.080
So let's say we have this
text from one of my tweets,

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:41.830
and I want to extract the
parts of speech from that text.

00:02:41.830 --> 00:02:44.457
This is a really common natural
language processing task,

00:02:44.457 --> 00:02:46.040
so I don't need to
start from scratch.

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:50.470
I can utilize an existing
NLP model to help me do this.

00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:52.150
But let's say, on
the other hand,

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:53.940
that I want to take
the same tweet.

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:56.100
And I want my model
to output these tags.

00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:57.600
I want my model
to know that this

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:00.450
is a tweet about programming,
and more specifically

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:02.410
is a tweet about Google Cloud.

00:03:02.410 --> 00:03:05.490
I'm gonna need to train my
model on thousands of tweets

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:07.410
about each of these
things so that it

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:08.850
can generate these predictions.

00:03:11.414 --> 00:03:13.330
So many people see the
term, machine learning,

00:03:13.330 --> 00:03:15.010
and they're a little
bit scared off.

00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:17.800
They think it's something
that's only for experts.

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:20.440
Now, if we look back
about 60 years ago,

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:21.760
this was definitely the case.

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:24.520
This is a picture of the
first neural network invented

00:03:24.520 --> 00:03:27.130
in 1957 called a perceptron.

00:03:27.130 --> 00:03:29.410
And this was a device that
demonstrated an ability

00:03:29.410 --> 00:03:31.880
to identify different shapes.

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:34.820
So back then, if you wanted
to work on machine learning,

00:03:34.820 --> 00:03:37.870
you needed access to extensive
academic and computing

00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:39.895
resources.

00:03:39.895 --> 00:03:42.500
But if we fast
forward to today, we

00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:44.854
can see that just in the
last five or 10 years,

00:03:44.854 --> 00:03:47.520
the number of products at Google
that are using machine learning

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:50.530
has grown dramatically.

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:53.250
And at Google, we want
to put machine learning

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:56.010
into the hands of any
developer and data scientist

00:03:56.010 --> 00:03:58.461
with a computer and a
machine learning problem

00:03:58.461 --> 00:03:59.460
that they want to solve.

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:00.690
That's all of you.

00:04:00.690 --> 00:04:01.740
We don't think machine
learning should

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:03.015
be something only for experts.

00:04:05.047 --> 00:04:07.630
So if you think about how you're
doing machine learning today,

00:04:07.630 --> 00:04:10.150
maybe you're using a
framework like Scikit-learn,

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:13.390
XGBoost, Keras, or
TensorFlow, maybe you're

00:04:13.390 --> 00:04:16.360
writing your code in
Jupyter Notebooks,

00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:19.300
maybe you're just experimenting
with different types of models,

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:21.700
building proofs of
concept, or maybe you've

00:04:21.700 --> 00:04:23.500
already built a model
and you're working

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:25.580
on scaling it for production.

00:04:25.580 --> 00:04:28.330
So what I want you to take
away from this talk is that

00:04:28.330 --> 00:04:31.310
no matter what your existing
machine learning toolkit is,

00:04:31.310 --> 00:04:34.790
we've got something to help
you on Google Cloud Platform.

00:04:34.790 --> 00:04:37.140
And that's what I'm gonna
talk to you about today.

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:39.950
So we have a whole spectrum
of machine learning products

00:04:39.950 --> 00:04:41.000
on GCP.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:43.880
On the left-hand side,
we have products targeted

00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:45.770
towards application developers.

00:04:45.770 --> 00:04:49.490
To use these, you need little to
no machine learning experience.

00:04:49.490 --> 00:04:51.360
On the right-hand
side of the spectrum,

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:52.984
we have products that
are targeted more

00:04:52.984 --> 00:04:56.487
towards data scientists and
machine learning practitioners.

00:04:56.487 --> 00:04:58.320
So the first set of
products I'll talk about

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:00.390
is our machine learning APIs.

00:05:00.390 --> 00:05:03.210
These are APIs that give you
access to pre-trained models

00:05:03.210 --> 00:05:05.030
with a single REST API request.

00:05:07.824 --> 00:05:09.990
As we move toward the middle,
we have a new product,

00:05:09.990 --> 00:05:11.820
which I'm super
excited about, that we

00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:14.970
announced earlier this year
in January called AutoML.

00:05:14.970 --> 00:05:17.510
It's currently an alpha,
and the first AutoML product

00:05:17.510 --> 00:05:19.560
we've announced
is AutoML Vision.

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:22.020
So this let's you build a
custom image classification

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:25.350
model trained on your own
data without requiring you

00:05:25.350 --> 00:05:29.130
to write any of the model code.

00:05:29.130 --> 00:05:32.400
As we move further to the right,
towards more custom models,

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:35.182
if you want to build
your model in TensorFlow,

00:05:35.182 --> 00:05:37.140
we have a service called
Cloud Machine Learning

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:42.030
Engine to let you train and
serve your model at scale.

00:05:42.030 --> 00:05:44.610
Then a couple of months ago,
we announced an open source

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:46.560
project called Kubeflow.

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:48.780
This lets you run your
machine learning workloads

00:05:48.780 --> 00:05:51.990
on Kubernetes.

00:05:51.990 --> 00:05:54.920
And then finally, our most
do-it-yourself solution

00:05:54.920 --> 00:05:56.670
is-- let's say you
have a machine learning

00:05:56.670 --> 00:05:59.490
framework other than TensorFlow
and you want to run it on GCP.

00:05:59.490 --> 00:06:02.430
You can do this using Google
Compute Engine or Google

00:06:02.430 --> 00:06:04.140
Kubernetes Engine.

00:06:04.140 --> 00:06:05.820
But we don't have
tons of time today,

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:09.270
so we're going to focus
just on three of these.

00:06:09.270 --> 00:06:11.550
And let's dive right in,
starting with machine

00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:12.675
learning as an API.

00:06:15.260 --> 00:06:17.860
On Google Cloud Platform,
we have five APIs

00:06:17.860 --> 00:06:19.610
that give you access
to pre-trained models

00:06:19.610 --> 00:06:22.320
to accomplish common
machine learning tasks.

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:25.670
So they let you do things like
analyzing images, analyzing

00:06:25.670 --> 00:06:28.370
videos, converting
audio to text,

00:06:28.370 --> 00:06:32.240
analyzing that text further,
and then translating that text.

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:34.290
I'm going to show you
just two of them today.

00:06:34.290 --> 00:06:36.770
So let's start
with Cloud Vision.

00:06:36.770 --> 00:06:39.170
This is everything the
Vision API lets you do.

00:06:39.170 --> 00:06:42.410
So at its core, the Vision
API provides label detection.

00:06:42.410 --> 00:06:44.570
This will tell you-- what
is this a picture of?

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:47.260
So for this image, it might
return elephant, animal,

00:06:47.260 --> 00:06:48.950
et cetera.

00:06:48.950 --> 00:06:50.870
Web deception goes
one step further.

00:06:50.870 --> 00:06:53.630
It will search the web
for similar images.

00:06:53.630 --> 00:06:56.780
And it will give you labels
based on what it finds.

00:06:56.780 --> 00:07:00.080
OCR stands for Optical
Character Recognition.

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:02.030
This will let you find
text in your images,

00:07:02.030 --> 00:07:05.840
tells you where that text is
and what language it's in.

00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:09.020
Logo detection will identify
common logos in an image.

00:07:09.020 --> 00:07:11.740
Landmark detection will
find common landmarks.

00:07:11.740 --> 00:07:15.062
Crop hints will suggest crop
dimensions for your photos.

00:07:15.062 --> 00:07:16.520
And then explicit
content detection

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:20.584
will tell you-- is this
image appropriate or not.

00:07:20.584 --> 00:07:22.750
This is what a request to
the Vision API looks like.

00:07:22.750 --> 00:07:24.500
So, again, you don't
need to know anything

00:07:24.500 --> 00:07:26.800
about how that pre-trained
model works under the hood.

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:29.530
You pass it either a URL to
your image in Google Cloud

00:07:29.530 --> 00:07:32.830
Storage or just the
base64 encoded image,

00:07:32.830 --> 00:07:35.230
and then you tell it what
types of feature detection

00:07:35.230 --> 00:07:37.566
you want to run.

00:07:37.566 --> 00:07:39.440
Now, it's just a REST
API, so you can call it

00:07:39.440 --> 00:07:40.564
in any language you'd like.

00:07:40.564 --> 00:07:43.520
I have an example in Python here
using our Google Cloud client

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:45.110
library for Python.

00:07:45.110 --> 00:07:47.262
I create an
ImageAnnotatorClient.

00:07:47.262 --> 00:07:49.595
And then I run label detection
and analyze the response.

00:07:52.112 --> 00:07:54.320
I'm guessing many of you
heard about ML Kit announced

00:07:54.320 --> 00:07:55.590
a couple days ago.

00:07:55.590 --> 00:07:57.650
So if you want to use
ML Kit for Firebase,

00:07:57.650 --> 00:07:59.600
you can easily
call the Vision API

00:07:59.600 --> 00:08:01.690
from your Android or iOS app.

00:08:01.690 --> 00:08:03.665
And this is an example
of calling it in Swift.

00:08:06.690 --> 00:08:08.440
So I don't like to get
too far into a talk

00:08:08.440 --> 00:08:09.664
without showing a live demo.

00:08:09.664 --> 00:08:11.080
So if we could
switch to the demo.

00:08:13.720 --> 00:08:15.510
And what we have here
is the product page

00:08:15.510 --> 00:08:17.040
for the Vision API.

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:19.470
Here we can upload
images and see what

00:08:19.470 --> 00:08:21.550
the Vision API response.

00:08:21.550 --> 00:08:23.740
So I'm going to
upload an image here.

00:08:23.740 --> 00:08:26.990
This is a selfie that
I took seeing Hamilton.

00:08:26.990 --> 00:08:27.870
I live in New York.

00:08:27.870 --> 00:08:30.270
I was super excited to have
scored tickets to Hamilton.

00:08:30.270 --> 00:08:31.728
And I wanted to
see what the Vision

00:08:31.728 --> 00:08:34.122
API said about my selfie.

00:08:34.122 --> 00:08:35.580
So let's see what
we get back here.

00:08:38.448 --> 00:08:40.309
It's cranking.

00:08:40.309 --> 00:08:42.230
Live demos, you never
know how they'll go.

00:08:42.230 --> 00:08:43.700
There we go, OK.

00:08:43.700 --> 00:08:46.860
So we can see that it
found my face in the image.

00:08:46.860 --> 00:08:48.977
So it's able to identify
where my face is,

00:08:48.977 --> 00:08:50.810
different features in
my face, and it's also

00:08:50.810 --> 00:08:52.650
able to detect emotion.

00:08:52.650 --> 00:08:54.560
So we can see that joy
is very likely here.

00:08:54.560 --> 00:08:57.800
I was super excited
to be seeing Hamilton.

00:08:57.800 --> 00:08:59.900
What I didn't notice at
first about this image

00:08:59.900 --> 00:09:02.186
is that it has some text in it.

00:09:02.186 --> 00:09:03.560
When I sent it to
the Vision API,

00:09:03.560 --> 00:09:05.476
I didn't even notice
that there was text here,

00:09:05.476 --> 00:09:07.430
but we can see that
the API, using OCR,

00:09:07.430 --> 00:09:12.182
is able to extract that
Playbill text from my image.

00:09:12.182 --> 00:09:13.640
And then finally
in the browser, we

00:09:13.640 --> 00:09:16.550
can see the entire JSON
response we get back

00:09:16.550 --> 00:09:17.980
from the Vision API.

00:09:17.980 --> 00:09:20.480
So this is a great way
to try out the API.

00:09:20.480 --> 00:09:22.034
Before you start
writing any code,

00:09:22.034 --> 00:09:23.450
upload your images
in the browser,

00:09:23.450 --> 00:09:25.033
see the type of
response you get back,

00:09:25.033 --> 00:09:26.480
and if it's right for your app.

00:09:26.480 --> 00:09:28.650
And I'll provide a link
to this at the end.

00:09:28.650 --> 00:09:30.050
So that is the Vision API.

00:09:30.050 --> 00:09:33.072
If we could go
back to the slides.

00:09:33.072 --> 00:09:35.030
Next, I'm gonna talk
about the Natural Language

00:09:35.030 --> 00:09:39.685
API, which lets you analyze text
with a single REST API request.

00:09:39.685 --> 00:09:41.060
So the first thing
it lets you do

00:09:41.060 --> 00:09:44.420
is extract key
entities from text.

00:09:44.420 --> 00:09:48.311
It also tells you whether your
text is positive or negative.

00:09:48.311 --> 00:09:50.810
And then if you want to get
more into the linguistic details

00:09:50.810 --> 00:09:54.119
of your text, you can use
the syntax analysis method.

00:09:54.119 --> 00:09:55.910
And finally, the newest
feature of this API

00:09:55.910 --> 00:09:57.730
is content classification.

00:09:57.730 --> 00:09:59.930
It will classify
your text into many

00:09:59.930 --> 00:10:05.320
of over 700 different categories
that we have available.

00:10:05.320 --> 00:10:07.787
Here is some Python code to
call the Natural Language API.

00:10:07.787 --> 00:10:10.120
It's going to look pretty
similar to the Vision API code

00:10:10.120 --> 00:10:11.622
we saw on the previous page.

00:10:11.622 --> 00:10:13.330
And again, we don't
need to know anything

00:10:13.330 --> 00:10:14.530
about how this model works.

00:10:14.530 --> 00:10:17.470
We just send it our text and get
back the result from the model.

00:10:20.470 --> 00:10:23.080
Now, let's jump to a demo
of the Natural Language API.

00:10:25.450 --> 00:10:27.950
So again, this is our products
page for the Natural Language

00:10:27.950 --> 00:10:28.970
API.

00:10:28.970 --> 00:10:32.900
And here we can enter text
directly in this textbox

00:10:32.900 --> 00:10:35.060
and see what the Natural
Language API responds.

00:10:35.060 --> 00:10:39.830
So I'm going to say,
"I loved Google I/O,

00:10:39.830 --> 00:10:44.207
but the ML talk was just OK."

00:10:44.207 --> 00:10:45.165
We'll see what it says.

00:10:45.165 --> 00:10:47.054
This is a review I
might find on a session.

00:10:47.054 --> 00:10:49.720
And let's say that I didn't want
to go through all the sessions,

00:10:49.720 --> 00:10:51.480
but I wanted to
extract key entities

00:10:51.480 --> 00:10:53.760
and see what the sentiment was.

00:10:53.760 --> 00:10:56.160
So we can see here it's
extracted two entities, Google

00:10:56.160 --> 00:11:00.000
I/O and ML talk, and we get
a score for each entity.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:03.270
The score is a value
from negative one to one

00:11:03.270 --> 00:11:05.680
that'll tell us overall
how positive or negative

00:11:05.680 --> 00:11:06.930
is a sentiment in this entity.

00:11:06.930 --> 00:11:10.320
So Google I/O got 0.8,
almost fully positive.

00:11:10.320 --> 00:11:13.680
We even get a link to the
Wikipedia page for Google I/O.

00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:15.960
And then ML talk got
a neutral score right

00:11:15.960 --> 00:11:19.350
in the middle of zero
because it was just OK.

00:11:19.350 --> 00:11:22.920
We also get the aggregate
sentiment for this sentence.

00:11:22.920 --> 00:11:25.760
And we can also look at
the syntax and details,

00:11:25.760 --> 00:11:28.980
see which words depend on other
words, get the parts of speech

00:11:28.980 --> 00:11:31.290
for all the words in our text.

00:11:31.290 --> 00:11:33.750
And then if our text was
longer than 20 words,

00:11:33.750 --> 00:11:36.240
we can make use of this
content categorization

00:11:36.240 --> 00:11:38.980
feature, which you can also
try right in the browser.

00:11:38.980 --> 00:11:40.920
And you can see a list
of all the categories

00:11:40.920 --> 00:11:43.060
that are available for that.

00:11:43.060 --> 00:11:45.594
So that is the
Natural Language API.

00:11:45.594 --> 00:11:47.010
If we could go
back to the slides.

00:11:50.939 --> 00:11:53.230
So I'm gonna talk briefly
about some companies that are

00:11:53.230 --> 00:11:55.410
using these APIs in production.

00:11:55.410 --> 00:11:58.570
Giphy is a website that
lets you search for GIFs

00:11:58.570 --> 00:12:00.640
and share them across the web.

00:12:00.640 --> 00:12:03.610
They use the Vision API's
optical character recognition

00:12:03.610 --> 00:12:06.860
feature to add search
by text functionality

00:12:06.860 --> 00:12:07.735
to all of their GIFs.

00:12:07.735 --> 00:12:10.480
So as you know, lots of
GIFs have text in them.

00:12:10.480 --> 00:12:13.400
Before they used the Vision API,
you couldn't search by text,

00:12:13.400 --> 00:12:16.210
and now they've dramatically
increased the accuracy

00:12:16.210 --> 00:12:18.160
of their search results.

00:12:18.160 --> 00:12:21.190
Hearst is a large
publishing company.

00:12:21.190 --> 00:12:23.410
And they're using the Natural
Language API's content

00:12:23.410 --> 00:12:26.230
categorization
method across over 30

00:12:26.230 --> 00:12:30.520
of their media properties to
categorize their news articles.

00:12:30.520 --> 00:12:33.370
Descript is a new app
that lets you transcribe

00:12:33.370 --> 00:12:34.960
meetings and interviews.

00:12:34.960 --> 00:12:38.900
And they're using the Speech
API for that transcription.

00:12:38.900 --> 00:12:42.470
So all of these three companies
are using just one API.

00:12:42.470 --> 00:12:44.560
We have also seen
a lot of examples

00:12:44.560 --> 00:12:47.440
of companies combining
different machine learning APIs.

00:12:47.440 --> 00:12:50.260
So Seenit is a crowd
source video platform

00:12:50.260 --> 00:12:52.570
that got thousands of
videos uploaded daily,

00:12:52.570 --> 00:12:55.405
so they have no time to
manually tag those videos.

00:12:55.405 --> 00:12:59.590
So they're using a combination
of Video Intelligence, Vision,

00:12:59.590 --> 00:13:02.500
Speech, and Natural Language
to automatically tag

00:13:02.500 --> 00:13:06.040
all of that video content that
they're seeing on the platform.

00:13:06.040 --> 00:13:07.930
Maslo is a new mobile app.

00:13:07.930 --> 00:13:09.370
It's an audio journaling app.

00:13:09.370 --> 00:13:12.430
So you can enter your journal
entries through audio.

00:13:12.430 --> 00:13:15.280
And they're using the Speech
API to transcribe that audio.

00:13:15.280 --> 00:13:17.140
And then they're using
the Natural Language

00:13:17.140 --> 00:13:20.050
API to extract key
entities, give you

00:13:20.050 --> 00:13:22.180
some insights about
your journal entries,

00:13:22.180 --> 00:13:24.880
and they're doing all that
processing with Cloud Functions

00:13:24.880 --> 00:13:27.610
and storing the
data in Firestore.

00:13:27.610 --> 00:13:32.020
So that is the
Natural Language API.

00:13:32.020 --> 00:13:34.540
So all of the products
I talked about so far

00:13:34.540 --> 00:13:37.430
have kind of abstracted
that model for you.

00:13:37.430 --> 00:13:39.970
And a lot of times when
I present on the APIs,

00:13:39.970 --> 00:13:42.280
people ask me, those
APIs sounds great,

00:13:42.280 --> 00:13:46.316
but what if I want to train
them on my own custom data?

00:13:46.316 --> 00:13:48.190
So we have this new
product that I mentioned,

00:13:48.190 --> 00:13:49.440
called AutoML Vision.

00:13:49.440 --> 00:13:50.940
It's currently an
alpha, so you need

00:13:50.940 --> 00:13:52.780
to be whitelisted to use it.

00:13:52.780 --> 00:13:55.240
This lets you train an
image classification model

00:13:55.240 --> 00:13:57.370
on your own image data.

00:13:57.370 --> 00:13:58.800
And this is best
seen with a demo,

00:13:58.800 --> 00:14:00.480
so I'm gonna introduce it first.

00:14:00.480 --> 00:14:04.000
So for this demo, let's say
that I'm a meteorologist.

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:06.430
Maybe I work at a
weather company.

00:14:06.430 --> 00:14:09.460
And I want to predict
weather trends and flight

00:14:09.460 --> 00:14:12.050
plans from images of clouds.

00:14:12.050 --> 00:14:15.910
So the obvious next question
is, can we use the cloud

00:14:15.910 --> 00:14:18.660
to analyze clouds?

00:14:18.660 --> 00:14:22.030
And as I've learned, there's
many, many different types

00:14:22.030 --> 00:14:27.260
of clouds, and they all indicate
different weather patterns.

00:14:27.260 --> 00:14:29.540
So when I first started
thinking about this demo,

00:14:29.540 --> 00:14:31.960
I thought, maybe I should
try the Vision API first

00:14:31.960 --> 00:14:34.490
and see what I get back.

00:14:34.490 --> 00:14:36.566
So as humans, if we look
at these two images,

00:14:36.566 --> 00:14:38.690
it's pretty obvious to us
that these are completely

00:14:38.690 --> 00:14:40.370
different types of clouds.

00:14:40.370 --> 00:14:42.610
The Vision API,
we wouldn't expect

00:14:42.610 --> 00:14:46.190
it to know specifically what
type of cloud these images are.

00:14:46.190 --> 00:14:49.250
It was trained across a
wide variety of images

00:14:49.250 --> 00:14:52.070
to recognize all sorts of
different products, categories,

00:14:52.070 --> 00:14:55.470
but nothing as specific as
cloud types for these images.

00:14:55.470 --> 00:14:57.320
So you can see, even
for these images

00:14:57.320 --> 00:14:59.840
of obviously different
clouds, we get back

00:14:59.840 --> 00:15:01.050
pretty similar labels--

00:15:01.050 --> 00:15:05.340
sky, cloud, blue, et cetera.

00:15:05.340 --> 00:15:08.230
So this is where AutoML
Vision comes in really handy.

00:15:08.230 --> 00:15:10.860
AutoML Vision provides
a UI to help us

00:15:10.860 --> 00:15:14.010
with every step of training
our machine learning model,

00:15:14.010 --> 00:15:17.040
from importing the data,
labeling it, training

00:15:17.040 --> 00:15:20.310
it, and then generating
predictions using a REST API.

00:15:20.310 --> 00:15:26.680
So the best way to see it is
by, again, jumping to a demo.

00:15:26.680 --> 00:15:30.420
So here we have the
UI for AutoML Vision.

00:15:30.420 --> 00:15:33.970
Again, it takes us through every
step of building our model.

00:15:33.970 --> 00:15:35.940
So the first step here
is importing our data.

00:15:35.940 --> 00:15:38.730
To do that, we put our data
in Google Cloud Storage,

00:15:38.730 --> 00:15:40.770
we put our images in
Google Cloud Storage,

00:15:40.770 --> 00:15:42.540
and then we just
create a CSV, where

00:15:42.540 --> 00:15:44.220
the first column
of the CSV is going

00:15:44.220 --> 00:15:47.400
to be the URL of our image,
and then the next column

00:15:47.400 --> 00:15:50.250
will be the label or labels
associated with that image.

00:15:50.250 --> 00:15:52.644
One image can have
multiple labels.

00:15:52.644 --> 00:15:54.060
So then we upload
our images here.

00:15:54.060 --> 00:15:57.210
I've already done
that for this example.

00:15:57.210 --> 00:15:59.760
Let me move over to
the Labeling tab.

00:15:59.760 --> 00:16:02.590
And in this model, I've got
five different types of clouds

00:16:02.590 --> 00:16:03.465
that I'm classifying.

00:16:03.465 --> 00:16:06.330
We can see them here, and
we can see how many images

00:16:06.330 --> 00:16:08.280
I have for each one.

00:16:08.280 --> 00:16:11.220
AutoML Vision only requires
10 images per label

00:16:11.220 --> 00:16:14.370
to start training, but
they recommend at least 100

00:16:14.370 --> 00:16:18.280
for high quality predictions.

00:16:18.280 --> 00:16:22.450
So the next step is to
review my Image labels.

00:16:22.450 --> 00:16:24.890
So I can see all my
cloud pictures here.

00:16:24.890 --> 00:16:26.780
I can see what label they are.

00:16:26.780 --> 00:16:30.290
If this one was incorrect,
I could go in here

00:16:30.290 --> 00:16:32.588
and switch it out.

00:16:32.588 --> 00:16:35.570
Well, let's say that I didn't
have time to label my data set,

00:16:35.570 --> 00:16:37.360
or I have a massive
image data set,

00:16:37.360 --> 00:16:38.920
I don't have time to label it.

00:16:38.920 --> 00:16:41.770
I can make use of this human
labeling service, which

00:16:41.770 --> 00:16:45.190
gives you access to a set of
in-house human labelers that

00:16:45.190 --> 00:16:46.940
will label your images for you.

00:16:46.940 --> 00:16:48.370
And then in just
a couple of days

00:16:48.370 --> 00:16:52.110
you'll get back a
labeled image data set.

00:16:52.110 --> 00:16:54.530
So the next part, once you've
got your labeled images,

00:16:54.530 --> 00:16:56.960
is to train your
model, and you can

00:16:56.960 --> 00:16:59.570
choose between a base
or advanced model.

00:16:59.570 --> 00:17:01.610
I'll talk about that
more in a moment.

00:17:01.610 --> 00:17:05.400
And to train it, all you do
is press this Train button.

00:17:05.400 --> 00:17:07.720
It's as simple as that.

00:17:07.720 --> 00:17:10.846
Once your model is trained,
you'll get an email.

00:17:10.846 --> 00:17:12.220
And the next thing
you want to do

00:17:12.220 --> 00:17:14.109
is you want to see how
this model performed

00:17:14.109 --> 00:17:16.630
using some common
machine learning metrics.

00:17:16.630 --> 00:17:18.849
So I'm not going to go
into all of them here,

00:17:18.849 --> 00:17:22.349
but I do want to highlight
the confusion matrix.

00:17:22.349 --> 00:17:25.360
So if it looks confusing, it's
called a confusion matrix,

00:17:25.360 --> 00:17:27.640
but let's take a look
at what it means.

00:17:27.640 --> 00:17:30.520
So for a good confusion matrix,
what we ideally want to see

00:17:30.520 --> 00:17:33.740
is a strong diagonal
from the top left.

00:17:33.740 --> 00:17:36.130
So what this is telling
us is when we uploaded

00:17:36.130 --> 00:17:38.350
our images to AutoML,
it automatically

00:17:38.350 --> 00:17:41.530
split our images for us
into training and testing.

00:17:41.530 --> 00:17:44.980
So it took most of our images,
used those to train the model,

00:17:44.980 --> 00:17:47.110
and then it reserved
a subset of our images

00:17:47.110 --> 00:17:49.960
to see how the model performed
on images that it had never

00:17:49.960 --> 00:17:51.340
seen before.

00:17:51.340 --> 00:17:54.850
So what this is telling us is
that for all of our altocumulus

00:17:54.850 --> 00:17:56.980
cloud images in our
test set, our model

00:17:56.980 --> 00:18:00.430
was able to identify
76% of them correctly,

00:18:00.430 --> 00:18:02.020
which is pretty good.

00:18:02.020 --> 00:18:06.160
Now on the Train tab, you saw
the base and advanced models,

00:18:06.160 --> 00:18:08.020
and I've actually trained both.

00:18:08.020 --> 00:18:10.720
So we're looking at the
advanced model here.

00:18:10.720 --> 00:18:13.770
I can use the UI to see how
different versions of my model

00:18:13.770 --> 00:18:16.210
performed and compare it.

00:18:16.210 --> 00:18:18.730
So we would expect that the
advanced model would perform

00:18:18.730 --> 00:18:20.090
better across the board.

00:18:20.090 --> 00:18:21.230
So let's take a look.

00:18:21.230 --> 00:18:25.510
So it looks like it did, indeed,
perform a lot better for most

00:18:25.510 --> 00:18:27.490
all of the categories here.

00:18:27.490 --> 00:18:31.386
You can see a 23% increase for
this one, 11% for this one,

00:18:31.386 --> 00:18:32.010
but, hey, wait.

00:18:32.010 --> 00:18:34.195
If we look at our
altostratus images,

00:18:34.195 --> 00:18:37.890
it looks like our advanced model
actually did worse on that.

00:18:37.890 --> 00:18:39.424
What this is
actually pointing out

00:18:39.424 --> 00:18:41.590
is that there may be some
problems with our training

00:18:41.590 --> 00:18:42.490
images here.

00:18:42.490 --> 00:18:44.562
So the advanced model
has done a better job

00:18:44.562 --> 00:18:46.270
of identifying where
there is potentially

00:18:46.270 --> 00:18:48.561
problems with our training
data set, because, remember,

00:18:48.561 --> 00:18:52.460
our model is only as good as the
training data that we give it.

00:18:52.460 --> 00:18:55.990
So if we look here, we see that
14% of our altostratus clouds

00:18:55.990 --> 00:18:58.270
are being mislabeled
as cumulus clouds.

00:18:58.270 --> 00:19:01.480
And if I click on this,
I can look and see

00:19:01.480 --> 00:19:04.180
where my model is
getting confused.

00:19:04.180 --> 00:19:05.920
And it turns out
that these are images

00:19:05.920 --> 00:19:08.530
that I could have potentially
labeled incorrectly.

00:19:08.530 --> 00:19:11.290
I'm not an expert
on actual clouds.

00:19:11.290 --> 00:19:13.000
Some of these images
have multiple types

00:19:13.000 --> 00:19:14.458
of clouds in them,
so they actually

00:19:14.458 --> 00:19:16.270
are pretty confusing images.

00:19:16.270 --> 00:19:18.250
So what the confusion
matrix can help me do

00:19:18.250 --> 00:19:21.040
is identify where I need to go
back and improve my training

00:19:21.040 --> 00:19:22.460
data.

00:19:22.460 --> 00:19:25.040
So that's the Evaluate tab.

00:19:25.040 --> 00:19:27.770
The next part, the most
fun part in my opinion,

00:19:27.770 --> 00:19:31.440
is generating
predictions on new data.

00:19:31.440 --> 00:19:35.210
So I'm going to take this
image of a cirrus cloud,

00:19:35.210 --> 00:19:37.790
and we're going to see
what our model predicts.

00:19:37.790 --> 00:19:40.040
Again, it has never
seen this image before.

00:19:40.040 --> 00:19:42.620
It wasn't used during training.

00:19:42.620 --> 00:19:45.520
And we'll see how it performs.

00:19:45.520 --> 00:19:46.020
There we go.

00:19:46.020 --> 00:19:49.190
So we can see that our
model is 99% confident

00:19:49.190 --> 00:19:52.550
this is a cirrus cloud, which
is pretty cool considering it's

00:19:52.550 --> 00:19:55.100
never seen this image before.

00:19:55.100 --> 00:19:57.355
So the UI is one way that
you can generate predictions

00:19:57.355 --> 00:19:58.730
once your model
has been trained.

00:19:58.730 --> 00:20:01.730
It's a good way to try it out
right after training completes.

00:20:01.730 --> 00:20:03.350
But chances are
you probably want

00:20:03.350 --> 00:20:06.020
to build an app that's going
to query your trained model.

00:20:06.020 --> 00:20:08.400
And there's a couple
different ways to do this.

00:20:08.400 --> 00:20:11.155
I want to highlight
the Vision API here.

00:20:11.155 --> 00:20:12.530
So if you remember
the Vision API

00:20:12.530 --> 00:20:14.970
request from a couple
of slides back,

00:20:14.970 --> 00:20:18.150
you'll notice that this
doesn't look much different.

00:20:18.150 --> 00:20:22.130
All I need to add is this custom
label detection parameter.

00:20:22.130 --> 00:20:26.120
And then once my model trains,
I get an ID for that trained

00:20:26.120 --> 00:20:28.760
model that only I have
access to or anybody

00:20:28.760 --> 00:20:30.890
that I've shared
my project with.

00:20:30.890 --> 00:20:33.680
So if I have an app,
let's say it's just

00:20:33.680 --> 00:20:36.130
detecting whether or not
there is a cloud in an image.

00:20:36.130 --> 00:20:38.300
Then let's say I want
to upgrade my app.

00:20:38.300 --> 00:20:39.860
I want to build a custom model.

00:20:39.860 --> 00:20:43.190
I don't have to change much at
all about my app architecture

00:20:43.190 --> 00:20:44.960
to now point it to
my custom model.

00:20:44.960 --> 00:20:49.230
I just need to modify the
request.JSON a little bit.

00:20:49.230 --> 00:20:51.650
So that is AutoML Vision.

00:20:51.650 --> 00:20:54.586
If we could go
back to the slides.

00:20:54.586 --> 00:20:55.960
A little bit about
some companies

00:20:55.960 --> 00:20:59.410
that are using AutoML Vision
and have been part of the alpha.

00:20:59.410 --> 00:21:01.450
Disney is the first example.

00:21:01.450 --> 00:21:04.480
They trained a model on
different Disney characters,

00:21:04.480 --> 00:21:06.742
product categories, and colors.

00:21:06.742 --> 00:21:08.950
And they're integrating that
into their search engine

00:21:08.950 --> 00:21:11.860
to give users more
accurate results.

00:21:11.860 --> 00:21:14.120
Urban Outfitters is
a clothing company.

00:21:14.120 --> 00:21:16.000
They've got a similar use case.

00:21:16.000 --> 00:21:17.380
They trained a
model to recognize

00:21:17.380 --> 00:21:21.010
different types of clothing
patterns and neckline styles.

00:21:21.010 --> 00:21:24.070
And they're using that to create
a comprehensive set of product

00:21:24.070 --> 00:21:24.970
attributes.

00:21:24.970 --> 00:21:27.550
And they're using it to
improve product recommendations

00:21:27.550 --> 00:21:30.130
and give users better
search results.

00:21:30.130 --> 00:21:33.790
The last example is the
Zoological Society of London.

00:21:33.790 --> 00:21:36.610
They've got cameras
deployed all over the wild.

00:21:36.610 --> 00:21:39.370
And they built a model
to automatically tag

00:21:39.370 --> 00:21:42.310
all of the wildlife that they're
seeing across those cameras

00:21:42.310 --> 00:21:44.830
so that they don't need
somebody to manually review it.

00:21:48.476 --> 00:21:50.350
So the two products I've
talked about so far,

00:21:50.350 --> 00:21:52.600
the APIs and AutoML,
have entirely

00:21:52.600 --> 00:21:54.279
abstracted the model from us.

00:21:54.279 --> 00:21:56.320
We don't know anything
about how that model works

00:21:56.320 --> 00:21:57.639
under the hood.

00:21:57.639 --> 00:21:59.680
But let's say you've got
a more custom prediction

00:21:59.680 --> 00:22:03.950
task that's specific to
your data set or use case.

00:22:03.950 --> 00:22:05.630
So one example is--

00:22:05.630 --> 00:22:08.384
let's say you have just launched
a new product at your company,

00:22:08.384 --> 00:22:10.300
you're seeing it posted
a lot on social media,

00:22:10.300 --> 00:22:12.760
and you want to identify
where in an image

00:22:12.760 --> 00:22:14.530
that product is located.

00:22:14.530 --> 00:22:16.810
You're gonna need to train
a model on your own data

00:22:16.810 --> 00:22:17.650
to do that.

00:22:17.650 --> 00:22:20.140
Or let's say you've got
a lot of logs coming in,

00:22:20.140 --> 00:22:23.410
and you want to analyze
those logs to find anomalies

00:22:23.410 --> 00:22:24.790
in your application.

00:22:24.790 --> 00:22:29.890
These would all require a custom
model trained on your own data.

00:22:29.890 --> 00:22:32.260
We've got two tools
to help you do this--

00:22:32.260 --> 00:22:35.260
TensorFlow for building your
models and Machine Learning

00:22:35.260 --> 00:22:38.160
Engine for training and
serving those models at scale.

00:22:40.980 --> 00:22:42.830
So from the beginning,
the Google Brain team

00:22:42.830 --> 00:22:44.510
wanted everyone in
the industry to be

00:22:44.510 --> 00:22:46.885
able to benefit from all of
the machine learning products

00:22:46.885 --> 00:22:47.820
they were working on.

00:22:47.820 --> 00:22:50.790
So they made TensorFlow an
open source project on GitHub.

00:22:50.790 --> 00:22:53.030
And the uptake has
been phenomenal.

00:22:53.030 --> 00:22:56.150
It is the most popular machine
learning project on GitHub.

00:22:56.150 --> 00:22:58.779
It has, I believe, over
90,000 GitHub stars.

00:22:58.779 --> 00:23:00.320
I actually need to
update this slide.

00:23:00.320 --> 00:23:03.416
It just crossed over
13 million downloads.

00:23:03.416 --> 00:23:04.790
And because it's
open source, you

00:23:04.790 --> 00:23:07.440
can train and serve your
TensorFlow models anywhere.

00:23:10.179 --> 00:23:11.970
So once you've built
your TensorFlow model,

00:23:11.970 --> 00:23:14.400
you need to think about
training it and then

00:23:14.400 --> 00:23:18.660
generating predictions at
scale, also known as serving.

00:23:18.660 --> 00:23:20.490
So if your app
becomes a major hit,

00:23:20.490 --> 00:23:23.040
you're getting thousands of
prediction requests per minute,

00:23:23.040 --> 00:23:24.456
you're going to
need to find a way

00:23:24.456 --> 00:23:27.876
to serve that model at scale.

00:23:27.876 --> 00:23:29.750
And again, because
TensorFlow is open source,

00:23:29.750 --> 00:23:32.320
you can train and serve your
TensorFlow models anywhere.

00:23:32.320 --> 00:23:34.330
But this talk is about
Google Cloud Platform,

00:23:34.330 --> 00:23:37.720
so I'm gonna talk about our
managed service for TensorFlow.

00:23:37.720 --> 00:23:39.280
So on Cloud Machine
Learning Engine,

00:23:39.280 --> 00:23:43.790
you can run distributed
training with GPUs and TPUs.

00:23:43.790 --> 00:23:46.600
TPUs are custom hardware
designed specifically

00:23:46.600 --> 00:23:49.112
to accelerate machine
learning workloads.

00:23:49.112 --> 00:23:51.070
And then you can also
deploy your trained model

00:23:51.070 --> 00:23:53.050
to Machine Learning
Engine, and then

00:23:53.050 --> 00:23:57.640
use the ML Engine API to access
scalable online and batch

00:23:57.640 --> 00:23:59.256
prediction for your model.

00:23:59.256 --> 00:24:00.880
One of the great
things about ML Engine

00:24:00.880 --> 00:24:02.019
is that there's no lock-in.

00:24:02.019 --> 00:24:04.060
So let's say that I want
to make use of ML Engine

00:24:04.060 --> 00:24:06.820
for training my model, but then
I want to download my model

00:24:06.820 --> 00:24:08.140
and serve it somewhere else.

00:24:08.140 --> 00:24:10.660
That's really easy to do, and
I can do the reverse as well.

00:24:13.340 --> 00:24:15.970
So I'm going to talk about
two different types of custom

00:24:15.970 --> 00:24:19.150
models using TensorFlow running
on Cloud Machine Learning

00:24:19.150 --> 00:24:19.965
Engine.

00:24:19.965 --> 00:24:22.090
The first type of custom
model I'm gonna talk about

00:24:22.090 --> 00:24:23.506
is transfer learning,
which allows

00:24:23.506 --> 00:24:26.980
us to update an existing trained
model using our own data.

00:24:26.980 --> 00:24:30.100
And then I'm gonna talk about
training a model from scratch

00:24:30.100 --> 00:24:32.810
using only your data.

00:24:32.810 --> 00:24:35.410
So transfer learning is great
if you need a custom model,

00:24:35.410 --> 00:24:37.970
but you don't have quite
enough training data.

00:24:37.970 --> 00:24:41.230
So what this lets us do is it
lets us utilize an existing

00:24:41.230 --> 00:24:43.390
pre-trained model that's
been trained on hundreds

00:24:43.390 --> 00:24:47.020
of thousands, maybe
millions, of images or text

00:24:47.020 --> 00:24:49.287
to do something similar to
what we're trying to do.

00:24:49.287 --> 00:24:51.370
And then we take the weights
of that trained model

00:24:51.370 --> 00:24:54.100
and update the last couple
of layers with our own data,

00:24:54.100 --> 00:24:57.790
so that the outcome is output
that's generating predictions

00:24:57.790 --> 00:25:00.930
on our own training data.

00:25:00.930 --> 00:25:03.090
So I wanted to build an
end-to-end example showing

00:25:03.090 --> 00:25:06.269
how to train a model and then
go all the way to serving

00:25:06.269 --> 00:25:08.310
it and building an app
that generates predictions

00:25:08.310 --> 00:25:09.330
against it.

00:25:09.330 --> 00:25:11.730
I know a little
bit of Swift, so I

00:25:11.730 --> 00:25:15.142
decided to build an iOS app that
could detect breeds of pets.

00:25:15.142 --> 00:25:16.850
This is a GIF of what
the app looks like.

00:25:16.850 --> 00:25:18.570
So you upload a
picture of your pet,

00:25:18.570 --> 00:25:20.730
it's able to detect where
the pet is an image,

00:25:20.730 --> 00:25:23.510
and what type of breed it is.

00:25:23.510 --> 00:25:26.480
To build that model, I use the
TensorFlow Object Detection

00:25:26.480 --> 00:25:29.720
API, which is a library that's
built on top of TensorFlow.

00:25:29.720 --> 00:25:32.000
It's open source to what
you do, specifically object

00:25:32.000 --> 00:25:35.150
detection, which is identifying
a bounding box of where

00:25:35.150 --> 00:25:37.910
something is in an image.

00:25:37.910 --> 00:25:40.290
I trained the model on
Machine Learning Engine.

00:25:40.290 --> 00:25:42.540
I also served it on
Machine Learning Engine.

00:25:42.540 --> 00:25:44.400
And then I used a
couple of Firebase APIs

00:25:44.400 --> 00:25:46.450
to build a frontend for my app.

00:25:46.450 --> 00:25:49.590
So this is a full
architecture diagram.

00:25:49.590 --> 00:25:53.190
And the iOS client is
actually a pretty thin client.

00:25:53.190 --> 00:25:55.530
What it's doing is
it's uploading images

00:25:55.530 --> 00:25:57.930
to Cloud Storage for Firebase.

00:25:57.930 --> 00:25:59.910
And then I've got a
Cloud Function that's

00:25:59.910 --> 00:26:01.680
listening on that
bucket, so that function

00:26:01.680 --> 00:26:04.470
is going to be triggered
anytime an image is uploaded.

00:26:04.470 --> 00:26:07.680
It then downloads the
image, base64 encodes it,

00:26:07.680 --> 00:26:09.894
sends it to Machine Learning
Engine for prediction.

00:26:09.894 --> 00:26:11.310
And then the
prediction that I get

00:26:11.310 --> 00:26:14.910
back is going to be a
confidence value, a label,

00:26:14.910 --> 00:26:18.660
and then bounding box data
for where exactly that pet is

00:26:18.660 --> 00:26:20.290
in my image.

00:26:20.290 --> 00:26:23.280
So then I write that new
image with the box around it

00:26:23.280 --> 00:26:25.620
to Cloud Storage,
and then I store

00:26:25.620 --> 00:26:28.320
the metadata in Firestore.

00:26:28.320 --> 00:26:31.900
So here's an example showing
how the frontend works.

00:26:31.900 --> 00:26:34.471
So this is a screenshot
of Firestore,

00:26:34.471 --> 00:26:36.720
and we can see that whenever
my image data is uploaded

00:26:36.720 --> 00:26:39.930
to Firestore, I write the new
image with the box around it

00:26:39.930 --> 00:26:41.490
to a Cloud Storage bucket.

00:26:45.800 --> 00:26:48.180
So that was an example
of transfer learning.

00:26:48.180 --> 00:26:50.510
But let's say that we
have a custom task,

00:26:50.510 --> 00:26:52.370
and we've got enough
data to build and train

00:26:52.370 --> 00:26:55.750
a model entirely from scratch.

00:26:55.750 --> 00:26:58.600
For this, I'm going to show you
a demo I built of a model that

00:26:58.600 --> 00:27:00.430
predicts the price of wine.

00:27:00.430 --> 00:27:03.850
So I wanted to see, given a
wine's description and variety,

00:27:03.850 --> 00:27:06.960
can we predict the price?

00:27:06.960 --> 00:27:09.530
So this is what an example input
and prediction for our model

00:27:09.530 --> 00:27:10.836
would be.

00:27:10.836 --> 00:27:12.710
So one reason this is
well-suited for machine

00:27:12.710 --> 00:27:16.040
learning is I can't
write rules to determine

00:27:16.040 --> 00:27:17.860
what the price of
this wine should be.

00:27:17.860 --> 00:27:20.240
So I can't say that
any wine with vanilla

00:27:20.240 --> 00:27:21.890
in the description
and Pinot noir

00:27:21.890 --> 00:27:25.177
is going to be a mid-price wine,
whereas a fruity chardonnay is

00:27:25.177 --> 00:27:26.510
always going to be a cheap wine.

00:27:26.510 --> 00:27:28.330
I can't write rules to do that.

00:27:28.330 --> 00:27:30.830
So I wanted to see if I could
build a machine learning model

00:27:30.830 --> 00:27:34.762
to extract insights
from this data.

00:27:34.762 --> 00:27:36.720
As I mentioned, because
I'm training this model

00:27:36.720 --> 00:27:38.850
from scratch, there's no
existing model out there

00:27:38.850 --> 00:27:41.730
that does exactly this task
that I want to perform.

00:27:41.730 --> 00:27:44.340
I'm gonna need a lot of data.

00:27:44.340 --> 00:27:45.900
I use Kaggle to get the data.

00:27:45.900 --> 00:27:48.270
Kaggle is a data
science platform.

00:27:48.270 --> 00:27:49.366
It's part of Google.

00:27:49.366 --> 00:27:51.240
If you're new to machine
learning and looking

00:27:51.240 --> 00:27:53.156
for interesting data
sets to play around with,

00:27:53.156 --> 00:27:55.290
I definitely recommend
checking out Kaggle.

00:27:55.290 --> 00:27:58.320
So Kaggle has this
wine reviews data set.

00:27:58.320 --> 00:28:02.490
I found this data set on
150,000 different kinds of wine.

00:28:02.490 --> 00:28:05.360
For each wine, it has a lot
of data on the description--

00:28:05.360 --> 00:28:07.860
the points rating,
the price, et cetera.

00:28:07.860 --> 00:28:10.800
So in this I'm just using
the description, the variety,

00:28:10.800 --> 00:28:12.165
and the price for this model.

00:28:15.000 --> 00:28:16.995
So the next step is
to choose the API

00:28:16.995 --> 00:28:19.080
I'm going to use to
build my model, which

00:28:19.080 --> 00:28:23.200
TensorFlow API, and then the
type of model I want to use.

00:28:23.200 --> 00:28:26.170
So I chose to use the
tf.keras API here.

00:28:26.170 --> 00:28:28.840
So Keras is an open-source
machine learning framework

00:28:28.840 --> 00:28:31.780
created by Francois Chollet,
who works at Google,

00:28:31.780 --> 00:28:35.470
and the TensorFlow API includes
a full implementation of Keras.

00:28:35.470 --> 00:28:37.930
So Keras is a higher-level
TensorFlow API

00:28:37.930 --> 00:28:40.990
that makes this easy for us to
define the layers of our model.

00:28:40.990 --> 00:28:43.300
You can also use a
lower-level TensorFlow API

00:28:43.300 --> 00:28:46.330
if you'd like more control
over the different operations

00:28:46.330 --> 00:28:48.160
in your model graph.

00:28:48.160 --> 00:28:51.160
So I chose to use
the tf.keras API,

00:28:51.160 --> 00:28:53.410
and then I chose to build
a wide and deep model

00:28:53.410 --> 00:28:54.780
to solve this task.

00:28:54.780 --> 00:28:56.890
Now wide and deep is
basically just a fancy way

00:28:56.890 --> 00:28:59.860
of saying I'm going to represent
the inputs to my model in two

00:28:59.860 --> 00:29:02.400
different ways.

00:29:02.400 --> 00:29:04.340
I'm going to use a
linear model, that's

00:29:04.340 --> 00:29:07.310
the wide part that's good
at memorizing relationships

00:29:07.310 --> 00:29:08.917
between different inputs.

00:29:08.917 --> 00:29:11.000
And then I'm going to use
a deep neural net, which

00:29:11.000 --> 00:29:13.700
is really good at
generalizing based on data

00:29:13.700 --> 00:29:16.530
that it hasn't seen before.

00:29:16.530 --> 00:29:19.080
Put it another way, the
input to our wide model

00:29:19.080 --> 00:29:21.750
is going to be
sparse wide vectors,

00:29:21.750 --> 00:29:23.610
and the input to our
deep model is going

00:29:23.610 --> 00:29:25.740
to be dense, embedding vectors.

00:29:25.740 --> 00:29:28.530
Now let's take a look at
what the code for our model

00:29:28.530 --> 00:29:30.280
will look like.

00:29:30.280 --> 00:29:33.045
So the first step, this is all
using the Keras functional API,

00:29:33.045 --> 00:29:35.220
is to define the wide model.

00:29:35.220 --> 00:29:37.610
And to represent my
text in my wide model,

00:29:37.610 --> 00:29:40.630
I'm gonna use what's called a
bag of words representation.

00:29:40.630 --> 00:29:43.470
So what this does is it takes
all the words that occur across

00:29:43.470 --> 00:29:48.360
all my wine descriptions, and
I chose to use the top 12,000.

00:29:48.360 --> 00:29:50.970
This is kind of a hyperparameter
that you can tune.

00:29:50.970 --> 00:29:54.180
So each input to my model is
going to be a 12,000-element

00:29:54.180 --> 00:29:57.360
vector with ones and zeros,
indicating whether or not

00:29:57.360 --> 00:30:00.150
the word from my vocabulary
is present in that specific

00:30:00.150 --> 00:30:00.870
description.

00:30:00.870 --> 00:30:03.330
So it doesn't take into
account the order of words

00:30:03.330 --> 00:30:05.220
or the relationship
between words.

00:30:05.220 --> 00:30:07.800
This wide model just takes
into account whether or not

00:30:07.800 --> 00:30:09.930
this word from my
vocabulary is present

00:30:09.930 --> 00:30:12.000
in that specific description.

00:30:12.000 --> 00:30:14.490
So this type of input is
going to look like this.

00:30:14.490 --> 00:30:16.680
It's going to be a
12,000-element bag of words

00:30:16.680 --> 00:30:18.130
vector.

00:30:18.130 --> 00:30:21.400
The way I'm representing
my variety in my wide model

00:30:21.400 --> 00:30:22.110
is pretty simple.

00:30:22.110 --> 00:30:24.540
I've got 40 different wine
varieties in my data set,

00:30:24.540 --> 00:30:28.250
so this is just going to be
a 40-element one-hot vector,

00:30:28.250 --> 00:30:30.230
with each index in the
vector corresponding

00:30:30.230 --> 00:30:32.300
to a different variety of wine.

00:30:32.300 --> 00:30:35.730
Then I'm going to concatenate
these input layers.

00:30:35.730 --> 00:30:38.090
And output to my
model is just a float

00:30:38.090 --> 00:30:40.600
indicating what the
price of that wine is.

00:30:40.600 --> 00:30:42.350
So if I just wanted
to use the wide model,

00:30:42.350 --> 00:30:44.240
I could take the wide
model that I have here

00:30:44.240 --> 00:30:47.210
and run training and
evaluation on it using Keras,

00:30:47.210 --> 00:30:49.550
but I found that I
had better accuracy

00:30:49.550 --> 00:30:52.489
when I combined wide and deep.

00:30:52.489 --> 00:30:54.280
So the next step is to
build my deep model.

00:30:54.280 --> 00:30:56.030
And for my deep
model, what I'm using

00:30:56.030 --> 00:30:58.340
is called an embedding layer.

00:30:58.340 --> 00:31:00.860
What word embeddings
let us do is

00:31:00.860 --> 00:31:04.590
they define the relationships
between words in vector space.

00:31:04.590 --> 00:31:06.620
So words that are
similar to each other

00:31:06.620 --> 00:31:08.924
are going to be closer
together in vector space.

00:31:08.924 --> 00:31:11.090
There's lots of reading out
there on word embedding,

00:31:11.090 --> 00:31:13.580
so I'm not going to focus
on the details here.

00:31:13.580 --> 00:31:15.920
The way it works is I can
choose the dimensionality

00:31:15.920 --> 00:31:16.715
of that space.

00:31:16.715 --> 00:31:18.840
Again, that's another
hyperparameter of this model,

00:31:18.840 --> 00:31:20.600
so that's something
I should tune and see

00:31:20.600 --> 00:31:22.230
what works best for my data set.

00:31:22.230 --> 00:31:24.521
So in this case, you can see
I use an eight dimensional

00:31:24.521 --> 00:31:25.740
embedding space.

00:31:25.740 --> 00:31:29.570
And obviously I can't feed the
text directly into my model.

00:31:29.570 --> 00:31:33.140
I need to put it into a format
that the model can understand.

00:31:33.140 --> 00:31:37.670
So what I did was I encoded
each word as an integer.

00:31:37.670 --> 00:31:39.420
So this is what the
input to my deep model

00:31:39.420 --> 00:31:40.336
is going to look like.

00:31:40.336 --> 00:31:42.616
And obviously the inputs
need to be the same length,

00:31:42.616 --> 00:31:44.740
but not all of my descriptions
are the same length,

00:31:44.740 --> 00:31:47.070
so I'm using 170 as a length.

00:31:47.070 --> 00:31:49.980
None of my descriptions
are longer than 170 words.

00:31:49.980 --> 00:31:52.500
So if they're shorter,
I'll just pad that vector

00:31:52.500 --> 00:31:56.170
with zeros at the
end, as I did here.

00:31:56.170 --> 00:31:57.730
The output is going
to be the same.

00:31:57.730 --> 00:32:00.630
We're still
predicting the price.

00:32:00.630 --> 00:32:02.880
And then using the
Keras functional API,

00:32:02.880 --> 00:32:04.680
it's relatively
straightforward to then

00:32:04.680 --> 00:32:07.560
combine the outputs from
both of these models

00:32:07.560 --> 00:32:10.140
and then to find
my combined model.

00:32:10.140 --> 00:32:12.480
And so now it's time to
run training and evaluation

00:32:12.480 --> 00:32:13.620
on that model.

00:32:13.620 --> 00:32:15.970
I chose to do that using
Machine Learning Engine.

00:32:15.970 --> 00:32:18.510
So the first step here
is to package my code

00:32:18.510 --> 00:32:21.764
and put it in Google
Cloud Storage.

00:32:21.764 --> 00:32:22.680
This is pretty simple.

00:32:22.680 --> 00:32:25.350
So I put my model code
in a trainer directory,

00:32:25.350 --> 00:32:27.660
I put my wine data
in a data directory,

00:32:27.660 --> 00:32:29.640
and then I have
this setup.py file,

00:32:29.640 --> 00:32:31.615
which is defining
the dependencies

00:32:31.615 --> 00:32:35.230
that my model is going to use.

00:32:35.230 --> 00:32:37.300
And then to run
that training job,

00:32:37.300 --> 00:32:39.220
I can use gcloud, which
is our Google Cloud

00:32:39.220 --> 00:32:42.160
CLI, for interacting with a
number of different Google

00:32:42.160 --> 00:32:43.930
Cloud products.

00:32:43.930 --> 00:32:47.380
So I run this gcloud command
to kick off my training job,

00:32:47.380 --> 00:32:50.380
and then I save my model
file, which is a binary file,

00:32:50.380 --> 00:32:53.400
to Google Cloud Storage.

00:32:53.400 --> 00:32:56.400
And let's see a demo of
generating some predictions

00:32:56.400 --> 00:32:58.410
on that model.

00:32:58.410 --> 00:33:00.360
So for this demo, I'm
going to use this tool

00:33:00.360 --> 00:33:02.374
called Colab, Google Colab.

00:33:02.374 --> 00:33:03.790
I'll have a link
to it at the end.

00:33:03.790 --> 00:33:06.390
And this is a cloud-hosted
Jupyter Notebook

00:33:06.390 --> 00:33:08.310
that comes with a free GPU.

00:33:08.310 --> 00:33:09.810
And so here, I've
already trained m.

00:33:09.810 --> 00:33:13.260
Model, and what I've done
is I saved that model file

00:33:13.260 --> 00:33:14.880
in Google Cloud Storage here.

00:33:14.880 --> 00:33:17.580
And now I'm going to generate
some predictions to see how

00:33:17.580 --> 00:33:19.050
it performs on some raw data.

00:33:19.050 --> 00:33:21.660
Let me make this a
little bit bigger.

00:33:21.660 --> 00:33:24.240
So here, we're just
importing some libraries

00:33:24.240 --> 00:33:28.170
that we're going to
use, unloading my model.

00:33:28.170 --> 00:33:31.190
Next step is to load the
tokenizer for my model.

00:33:31.190 --> 00:33:33.899
So this is just an index
associating all the words

00:33:33.899 --> 00:33:35.190
in my vocabulary with a number.

00:33:35.190 --> 00:33:38.342
And then I'm loading
my variety encoder too.

00:33:38.342 --> 00:33:40.460
I'm gonna ignore that warning.

00:33:40.460 --> 00:33:44.310
And here, I'm going to
load in some raw data.

00:33:44.310 --> 00:33:46.730
So what I have here is data
on five different wines.

00:33:46.730 --> 00:33:49.010
I've got the description,
the variety, and then

00:33:49.010 --> 00:33:52.830
the associated price
for each of these wines.

00:33:52.830 --> 00:33:54.780
And I wanted to show
you what the input looks

00:33:54.780 --> 00:33:55.738
like for each of these.

00:33:55.738 --> 00:33:58.110
So now we need to
encode each of these

00:33:58.110 --> 00:34:01.260
into the wide and deep format
that our model is expecting.

00:34:01.260 --> 00:34:04.380
So to do that, we have
this vocabulary look-up.

00:34:04.380 --> 00:34:06.250
I'm just printing a
subset of it here.

00:34:06.250 --> 00:34:08.670
So this is going to be
12,000 elements long

00:34:08.670 --> 00:34:10.730
for the top 12,000
words in our vocabulary.

00:34:10.730 --> 00:34:12.780
And Keras has some
utilities to help

00:34:12.780 --> 00:34:15.179
us extract those top words.

00:34:15.179 --> 00:34:17.880
And then my variety encoder
is just a 40-element array

00:34:17.880 --> 00:34:20.699
that looks like this, with
each index corresponding

00:34:20.699 --> 00:34:22.409
to a different variety.

00:34:22.409 --> 00:34:27.190
So let's see what the input
to our wide model looks like.

00:34:27.190 --> 00:34:29.370
So the first thing is our text.

00:34:29.370 --> 00:34:30.870
And this is a bag
of words vector.

00:34:30.870 --> 00:34:34.050
So it's a 12,000-element vector
with zeros and ones indicating

00:34:34.050 --> 00:34:36.090
the presence or absence
of different words

00:34:36.090 --> 00:34:37.560
in our vocabulary.

00:34:37.560 --> 00:34:38.790
And then the variety matrix--

00:34:38.790 --> 00:34:40.350
I'm just printing it
out for the first one.

00:34:40.350 --> 00:34:42.989
So I believe that first one was
a Pinot noir, which corresponds

00:34:42.989 --> 00:34:44.739
with this index here.

00:34:44.739 --> 00:34:47.219
And if we look up here,
we can confirm that, yes,

00:34:47.219 --> 00:34:48.929
that was a Pinot noir.

00:34:48.929 --> 00:34:51.719
So that's the input
to our wide model.

00:34:51.719 --> 00:34:55.050
And then our deep model, we're
just encoding all of the words

00:34:55.050 --> 00:34:57.270
from that first
description as integers

00:34:57.270 --> 00:34:58.800
and padding it with
zeros since this

00:34:58.800 --> 00:35:01.169
wasn't a very long description.

00:35:01.169 --> 00:35:03.210
And then all I need to do
to generate predictions

00:35:03.210 --> 00:35:07.360
on my Keras model
is call .predict.

00:35:07.360 --> 00:35:08.820
And what I'm doing
here is I'm now

00:35:08.820 --> 00:35:10.470
going to loop through
those predictions

00:35:10.470 --> 00:35:13.599
and see how the model performed
compared to the actual price.

00:35:13.599 --> 00:35:15.390
So we can see that it
did a pretty good job

00:35:15.390 --> 00:35:18.780
on the first one,
46 compared to 48.

00:35:18.780 --> 00:35:20.910
This one was about $30
off, but it was still

00:35:20.910 --> 00:35:24.160
able to understand that this
was a relatively higher priced

00:35:24.160 --> 00:35:25.500
wine.

00:35:25.500 --> 00:35:28.720
And it did pretty well on
the rest of these as well.

00:35:28.720 --> 00:35:30.360
So I'll have a link
to this at the end.

00:35:30.360 --> 00:35:33.840
Colab requires zero setup, so
you can all go to this URL,

00:35:33.840 --> 00:35:35.310
enter your own
wine descriptions,

00:35:35.310 --> 00:35:37.410
and see how the model performs.

00:35:37.410 --> 00:35:39.974
If we can go back to the slides.

00:35:39.974 --> 00:35:42.140
I'm going to talk briefly
about a few companies that

00:35:42.140 --> 00:35:44.690
are using TensorFlow and
Machine Learning Engine

00:35:44.690 --> 00:35:46.940
in production to
build custom models.

00:35:46.940 --> 00:35:49.220
The first example
is Rolls-Royce.

00:35:49.220 --> 00:35:51.200
They're using a custom
TensorFlow model

00:35:51.200 --> 00:35:54.290
in their marine division
to identify and track

00:35:54.290 --> 00:35:58.460
the objects that a vessel
can encounter at sea.

00:35:58.460 --> 00:36:01.830
Ocado is the UK's largest
grocery delivery service.

00:36:01.830 --> 00:36:03.950
So as you can imagine,
they get tons and tons

00:36:03.950 --> 00:36:06.170
of customer support
emails every day.

00:36:06.170 --> 00:36:07.480
And they built a custom model.

00:36:07.480 --> 00:36:10.760
It's able to take those emails
and predict whether it requires

00:36:10.760 --> 00:36:13.130
an urgent response
or maybe the email

00:36:13.130 --> 00:36:14.559
doesn't require a
response at all.

00:36:14.559 --> 00:36:16.850
And they've been able to save
a lot of customer support

00:36:16.850 --> 00:36:19.500
time using that model.

00:36:19.500 --> 00:36:21.950
And then finally, Airbus
has built a custom image

00:36:21.950 --> 00:36:26.168
classification model to identify
things in satellite imagery.

00:36:29.310 --> 00:36:31.590
So I know I covered a ton
of different products.

00:36:31.590 --> 00:36:34.540
I wanted to give you a
summary of what resources

00:36:34.540 --> 00:36:35.845
all of these products require.

00:36:35.845 --> 00:36:37.470
So these are just
four resources that I

00:36:37.470 --> 00:36:39.190
thought of that
you need to solve

00:36:39.190 --> 00:36:40.320
a machine learning problem.

00:36:40.320 --> 00:36:42.640
There's probably more
that I don't have on here.

00:36:42.640 --> 00:36:43.920
First is training data.

00:36:43.920 --> 00:36:45.336
How much training
data do you need

00:36:45.336 --> 00:36:46.830
to provide to train your model?

00:36:46.830 --> 00:36:48.949
How much model code do you need?

00:36:48.949 --> 00:36:50.740
How much training or
serving infrastructure

00:36:50.740 --> 00:36:52.050
do you need to provision?

00:36:52.050 --> 00:36:55.260
And then overall, how long is
this task going to take you?

00:36:55.260 --> 00:36:56.910
So if we look at
our Machine Learning

00:36:56.910 --> 00:36:58.950
APIs, great thing
about these-- you

00:36:58.950 --> 00:37:00.360
don't need any training data.

00:37:00.360 --> 00:37:03.240
You can just start generating
prediction on one image.

00:37:03.240 --> 00:37:05.315
You don't need to write
any of the model code.

00:37:05.315 --> 00:37:07.440
You don't need to provision
any training or serving

00:37:07.440 --> 00:37:08.730
infrastructure.

00:37:08.730 --> 00:37:11.880
And you can probably get started
with these in less than a day.

00:37:11.880 --> 00:37:14.085
Very little time.

00:37:14.085 --> 00:37:17.067
If we move to AutoML, the
cool thing about AutoML

00:37:17.067 --> 00:37:19.400
is that you will provide some
of your own training data,

00:37:19.400 --> 00:37:21.149
because you're going
to be creating a more

00:37:21.149 --> 00:37:22.560
customized model.

00:37:22.560 --> 00:37:24.325
And it will take
a little more time

00:37:24.325 --> 00:37:26.450
because you will need to
spend some time processing

00:37:26.450 --> 00:37:29.420
those images, uploading
them to the Cloud,

00:37:29.420 --> 00:37:30.680
and maybe labeling them.

00:37:30.680 --> 00:37:34.010
You still don't need any model
code or any training or serving

00:37:34.010 --> 00:37:36.930
infrastructure.

00:37:36.930 --> 00:37:39.960
And then finally, if we think
about a custom model, built

00:37:39.960 --> 00:37:42.360
with TensorFlow, running
on Cloud ML Engine,

00:37:42.360 --> 00:37:44.650
you will need a lot
of training data.

00:37:44.650 --> 00:37:47.121
You will have to write a lot
of the model code yourself.

00:37:47.121 --> 00:37:48.870
And you'll need to
think about whether you

00:37:48.870 --> 00:37:51.141
want to run your training
and serving jobs on premise,

00:37:51.141 --> 00:37:53.640
or if you want to use a managed
service like Cloud ML Engine

00:37:53.640 --> 00:37:55.020
to do that for you.

00:37:55.020 --> 00:37:57.570
And obviously this process will
take a little bit more time.

00:38:00.080 --> 00:38:02.320
Finally, so if you
remember only three things

00:38:02.320 --> 00:38:05.110
from this presentation--
so I know I covered a lot.

00:38:05.110 --> 00:38:08.410
First thing, you can
use a pre-trained API

00:38:08.410 --> 00:38:11.170
to accomplish common machine
learning tasks, like image

00:38:11.170 --> 00:38:15.550
analysis, natural language
processing, or translation.

00:38:15.550 --> 00:38:18.880
Second thing, if you want to
build an image classification

00:38:18.880 --> 00:38:22.607
API trained on your own
data, use AutoML Vision.

00:38:22.607 --> 00:38:24.940
I'm really interested to hear
if you have a specific use

00:38:24.940 --> 00:38:26.470
case for AutoML Vision.

00:38:26.470 --> 00:38:27.290
Come find me after.

00:38:27.290 --> 00:38:31.530
I will be in the
Cloud Sandbox area.

00:38:31.530 --> 00:38:34.080
Last thing, for custom
machine learning tasks,

00:38:34.080 --> 00:38:36.660
you can build a TensorFlow
model with your own data

00:38:36.660 --> 00:38:41.320
and train and serve it on
Machine Learning Engine.

00:38:41.320 --> 00:38:43.956
So here is a lot of great
resources covering everything

00:38:43.956 --> 00:38:44.830
I talked about today.

